export const POSTS = {
  "blueline-v0-2-0": {
    "slug": "blueline-v0-2-0",
    "title": "Your agent installs code nobody reads",
    "tag": "BLUELINE · V0.2.0",
    "date": "AUG 22, 2026",
    "desc": "Blueline v0.2.0, the release where the tool becomes a product. Review every npm install before it lands on your machine.",
    "withProgress": true,
    "withCopyMarkdown": true,
    "backHref": "/blog.html",
    "backLabel": "Back to blog",
    "body": "\n        <p>Blueline v0.2.0 is out, and it's the release where the tool becomes a product. One command and it's on your machine:</p>\n\n        <pre><code>npm i -g blueline\nnpx blueline install express@4.21.2</code></pre>\n\n        <p>Prebuilt binaries for linux (x64 glibc and musl, arm64), macOS (x64, arm64) and Windows (x64, arm64). No Rust toolchain required.</p>\n\n        <h2>The problem hasn't changed</h2>\n\n        <p>Your coding agent runs <code>npm install</code> dozens of times a day. Each one pulls tarballs off the registry and executes whatever install scripts came along for the ride. Nobody on earth diffs express 4.21.1 against 4.21.2 when an agent fires the command in a loop.</p>\n\n        <p>Attackers know this. The TanStack router worm shipped malicious npm packages carrying valid SLSA L3 provenance. In 2021, ua-parser-js was hijacked and three malicious versions published with a credential stealer in the preinstall script. The fix window was four hours. Agents compress that window to minutes.</p>\n\n        <p>Package managers treat installs as transport: fetch, unpack, run. Blueline adds the step they skip.</p>\n\n        <h2>What it does</h2>\n\n        <p>Blueline extracts the release tarball in an isolated sandbox, computes what changed against your last approved version, and shows you before anything lands:</p>\n\n        <pre><code>BLUELINE REVIEW CARD\n─────────────────────────────────────────\npackage:    express\nversion:    4.21.2\nprevious:   4.21.1 (last known-clean)\n\ndelta:      +3 files, -1 file\nsize:       +1.2 KB\nauthor:     expressjs (verified)\npermissions: no new binaries\ninstall script: none\n\nchanged:    lib/router/index.js (3 lines)\n            lib/router/layer.js (1 line, comment only)\n            package.json (version bump)\n\nverdict:    ▶ LOW RISK\n\n[a]pprove · [h]old · [d]iff</code></pre>\n\n        <p>Approve once and that version becomes the baseline. The next release only has to explain its delta. If risk thresholds trip, the install blocks and the workflow halts.</p>\n\n        <p>Around that core loop:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n          <li><code>blueline mcp</code>, an MCP server so agents call the review themselves instead of bypassing it</li>\n          <li><code>blueline ci</code>, a lockfile diff scanner for PRs, plus a GitHub Action</li>\n          <li>policy-as-code via <code>blueline.toml</code>: advisory thresholds, allowlists, blocklists</li>\n          <li>OSV and GitHub Advisory checks, SLSA provenance surfacing</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h2>Why you can trust the installer itself</h2>\n\n        <p>A supply chain tool delivered through the same supply chain it criticizes should hold itself to its own standard. v0.2.0 does:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n          <li>Every binary publishes as an npm package with provenance attestation.</li>\n          <li>SHA256SUMS attach to each GitHub release.</li>\n          <li>A smoke gate runs before shims publish: the actual shipped artifact must review a real package end-to-end through the npm path. If the artifact can't do its one job, nothing ships.</li>\n          <li>Our own composite Action reviews our own <code>package-lock.json</code> on every pull request.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <p>The scanner also fails closed. Any doubt in parsing, extraction, or verification errors out rather than guessing. We keep mutation tests pointed at those boundaries so a regression fails CI instead of failing you.</p>\n\n        <h2>Try it</h2>\n\n        <pre><code>npm i -g blueline\nblueline review lodash@4.17.21</code></pre>\n\n        <p>Point your agent at the MCP server, or drop the GitHub Action into a repo and let it review lockfile changes on every PR.</p>\n\n        <p>Repo and docs: <a href=\"https://github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/blueline\" class=\"post-link\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/blueline</a></p>\n\n        <p>Honest limits: first installs have no baseline to diff against, so they lean harder on advisories, provenance, and static scanning. Heuristics catch common obfuscation routes but are not proof against everything. If you want lower friction while bootstrapping baselines, there's an <code>allow_unreviewed_baseline</code> rule in <code>blueline.toml</code>.</p>\n\n        <p>Full case study: <a href=\"../projects/blueline.html\" class=\"post-link\">Blueline: approve the change, not the download</a></p>\n      "
  },
  "em-dashes-suck": {
    "slug": "em-dashes-suck",
    "title": "Asur, Shaitaan, Lucifer and the em-dash",
    "tag": "DEV · ESSAY",
    "date": "SEP 19, 2026",
    "desc": "Why em-dashes suck, how Claude and ChatGPT poisoned modern prose, and how AI slop writing summoned a new brand of hell: Asur, Shaitaan, Lucifer and the em-dash.",
    "withProgress": true,
    "withCopyMarkdown": true,
    "backHref": "/blog.html",
    "backLabel": "Back to blog",
    "body": "\n        <p>Open any tech essay published this week. Hit <code>Ctrl+F</code>. Type the em-dash character. Watch the browser light up like a slot machine hitting jackpot.</p>\n\n        <p>A few months ago, an essay went viral on X because someone ran that exact search. The document contained exactly sixty-seven em-dashes in a twelve-hundred word article. That is one dash every eighteen words. You could not read two sentences without tripping over a long horizontal stick propping up some limp, unearned thought.</p>\n\n        <p>The web is drowning in synthetic slime. Bad writing used to mean someone tried too hard, wrote purple prose, or misspelled a word. Now it means something far worse: reading an endless stream of smooth, rhythmically dead corporate sludge that sounds like an over-caffeinated product manager practicing throat-clearing in an empty auditorium.</p>\n\n        <p>Every mythology has its darkness. Asur in Vedic lore, Shaitaan in the burning desert winds, Lucifer falling from the heavens into ice. But none of those ancient devils prepared us for the modern demon haunting our text editors: the em-dash.</p>\n\n        <p>Dante mapped out seven circles of hell for ordinary human sinners. The demons had to dig an eighth gate exclusively for AI slop.</p>\n\n        <h2>1. The 67-dash autopsy</h2>\n\n        <p>The viral post with sixty-seven em-dashes was not an anomaly. It was the natural destination of modern language models left unsupervised.</p>\n\n        <p>People blame ChatGPT because it was the first giant hammer. In November 2025, Sam Altman posted on X celebrating a custom instructions update. His message was plain: if you tell ChatGPT to stop using em-dashes, it finally listens. Tech leaders were throwing themselves a party because their multi-billion-dollar system finally learned how to stop vomiting a single punctuation mark.</p>\n\n        <h3>Claude holds the crown</h3><p>Yet ChatGPT is not even the worst offender. Anthropic's Claude models hold that crown. Claude models have an absolute, pathological obsession with em-dashes. Claude loves them with a ferocity that defies logic.</p>\n\n        <p>Tell Claude in your system prompt: \"Do not use em-dashes.\" Claude nods, agrees, and then spits out double-hyphens instead. It substitutes the character code while keeping the exact same broken syntactic cadence. The model refuses to let go of the pause. Its weights are so warped by its training priors that it cannot imagine concluding an idea without tacking on a decorative tail.</p>\n\n        <h2>2. The mechanics of cowardice</h2>\n\n        <p>Why do models do this? It is not an accident. It is the direct result of Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback and Direct Preference Optimization.</p>\n\n        <h3>Trained to fear the blunt sentence</h3><p>Human raters sitting in labeling farms get tired. When a model writes a short, blunt statement, some rater thinks it sounds aggressive or underspecified. When a model writes a long, compound sentence, it risks making a factual error or losing grammatical agreement. Reward models learn to penalize both extremes.</p>\n\n        <p>The model finds an escape hatch: the em-dash. An em-dash lets an algorithm paste an extra clause onto a sentence without having to think about grammar. The model does not need to choose a coordinating conjunction. It does not need to decide between causal relationships. It just drops a stick on the screen, dumps a qualifying hedge, and runs away.</p>\n\n        <h3>The default rhythm</h3><p>Every frontier model defaults to:</p>\n\n        <pre><code>[Unprovable assertion] -> [hedged clarification] -> [meaningless sunny takeaway]</code></pre>\n\n        <p>Every paragraph follows this cadence. The model makes a claim, immediately gets scared of its own claim, inserts a dash to soften the blow, and finishes by reminding you that balance is vital. It is pure intellectual cowardice codified into floating-point numbers.</p>\n\n        <h2>3. Satan's eighth gate: the slop pentad</h2>\n\n        <p>The dash is only the visible symptom. The actual disease is the complete breakdown of human voice. Over the last two years, automated prose settled into five rigid pillars of slop.</p>\n\n        <h3>The five pillars of slop</h3><p><strong>First, the dash addiction.</strong> The crutch that connects clauses because the writer lacks the discipline to end a sentence.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>Second, the negative parallelism.</strong> Every AI essay contains some variation of this exact construction: <em>\"It is not just about building code; it is about reshaping the future of collaboration.\"</em> Nobody talks like this. Real engineers say: \"We built a tool that stops bad installs.\" Synthetic writing refuses to state what a tool does without first lecturing you on what it transcends.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>Third, the rule of three.</strong> Models refuse to give you two examples or four points. Everything arrives in an artificial triad. If an LLM describes an API, the API is always <em>\"fast, scalable, and intuitive.\"</em> If it describes an outage, the team responded with <em>\"urgency, precision, and focus.\"</em> It is a cheap rhetorical trick designed to create the illusion of balance where no real thought exists.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>Fourth, the jargon explosion.</strong> Certain words exist exclusively in LLM generation and corporate slide decks. Tapestry. Substrate. Delve. Pivot. Foster. Leverage. Intricate. Testament. Beacon. Nobody in an engineering standup says: \"Let us delve into this database migration to foster synergy across our memory substrate.\" If someone said that in a code review, you would check their pulse.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>Fifth, beating around the bush.</strong> Models hate the copula verb \"to be\". A model will never write: \"This file is thirty lines long.\" It will write: \"This file serves as a testament to concise implementation, standing as a beacon for maintainable architecture.\" It adds twenty empty words to avoid stating a physical reality.</p>\n\n        <h2>4. The collateral damage: paranoia</h2>\n\n        <p>The tragedy of this slop flood is the wreckage left for actual writers.</p>\n\n        <p>The em-dash used to belong to Emily Dickinson, Virginia Woolf, and classic American essayists. It was a stylistic device for sudden disruptions of thought, dramatic shifts, and breathless rhythm. Now, if a human writer uses two dashes in a tech blog, readers immediately open DevTools, accuse them of copy-pasting Claude output, and close the tab.</p>\n\n        <h3>The authenticity test</h3><p>Paranoid readers now use search as an authenticity test. If an article has three dashes and the word \"delve\", the reader discards it as machine junk. In response, human writers are actively censoring their own punctuation. Authors spend their editing passes stripping out dashes, eliminating parallel clauses, and dumbing down their natural rhythm just to prove they have a heartbeat.</p>\n\n        <p>We reached a stage where humans have to write awkwardly just to avoid sounding like machines that were trained on humans.</p>\n\n        <h2>5. Kill the dash: the unslop cure</h2>\n\n        <p>The solution is not another system prompt, another detector, or another fine-tuned classifier. The solution is writing with a spine.</p>\n\n        <h3>The rules we follow</h3>\n\n        <ul>\n          <li><strong>Zero em-dashes.</strong> None. No Unicode dashes, no double-hyphens, no clever substitutions. If a thought needs separation, end the sentence with a period. If the two clauses belong together, use a comma.</li>\n          <li><strong>Cut the negative parallelisms.</strong> State what you built. Do not tell the reader what it is not before telling them what it is.</li>\n          <li><strong>Kill the triads.</strong> Give the exact number of reasons that actually matter. If there is only one reason, write one. If there are four, list four.</li>\n          <li><strong>Ban the jargon words.</strong> Search your draft for delve, foster, tapestry, and substrate. Delete every single one. Replace them with plain concrete verbs.</li>\n          <li><strong>Say the concrete thing.</strong> Give numbers, file paths, and actual measurements. Describe what the code does on disk, not how it makes your workflow feel.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h3>Writing is thinking</h3><p>Writing is thinking. When you outsource your cadence to a language model and let it paste sixty-seven dashes across your page, you are not saving time. You are announcing that you had nothing to say in the first place.</p>\n      "
  },
  "multi-agent-git-chaos": {
    "slug": "multi-agent-git-chaos",
    "title": "Multi-agent coding loops will wreck your git history",
    "tag": "OOT · GOVERNANCE",
    "date": "AUG 29, 2026",
    "desc": "Why concurrent coding agents break git repos and how intent verification prevents silent merge regressions.",
    "withProgress": true,
    "withCopyMarkdown": true,
    "backHref": "/blog.html",
    "backLabel": "Back to blog",
    "body": "\n        <p>Everyone is excited to spin up five autonomous subagents in parallel to refactor a codebase. The reality is that Git was designed in 2005 for humans who read diffs, communicate asynchronously, and think before pushing.</p>\n\n        <p>When you let multiple autonomous models edit code concurrently in one repository, you get silent regressions that line-based merge engines cannot detect.</p>\n\n        <h2>The illusion of clean merges</h2>\n\n        <p>Git uses three-way line merges. If Agent A touches lines 10 through 25 of a file to refactor an auth method, and Agent B touches lines 80 through 95 to update a database query, Git declares a clean merge without conflicts.</p>\n\n        <p>Git does not know that Agent A renamed a session token type, while Agent B introduced a query that relies on the old token interface. Both agents run unit tests in isolation on their branch and report green. When merged, the build breaks, or worse, silently behaves incorrectly at runtime.</p>\n\n        <h2>The rebase death loop</h2>\n\n        <p>When agents detect a conflict, the default instinct is to rebase. In practice, here is what happens:</p>\n\n        <ol>\n          <li>Agent A finishes work on branch <code>feat/cache</code> and pushes to main.</li>\n          <li>Agent B was halfway through <code>feat/rate-limit</code>. It runs <code>git pull --rebase</code>.</li>\n          <li>Git pauses on a conflict. Agent B reads the conflict markers and attempts to resolve them.</li>\n          <li>Because Agent B has a narrow context window, it deletes Agent A's changes to make the local test suite pass.</li>\n          <li>Agent B force-pushes, completely erasing Agent A's work from the repository history.</li>\n        </ol>\n\n        <p>This is not a theoretical edge case. It happens constantly in multi-agent swarms.</p>\n\n        <h2>Intent verification over textual diffs</h2>\n\n        <p>To run multiple agents safely, we need semantic governance at the version control layer:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n          <li><strong>Intent locks:</strong> Agents must declare which symbols, AST nodes, and API contracts they are modifying before generating code.</li>\n          <li><strong>Semantic merge policies:</strong> Merges must verify type integrity and AST boundaries, not just line numbers.</li>\n          <li><strong>Audit trails:</strong> Every automated merge must record the reasoning and invariants expected by both agents.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <p>This is why I started building <a href=\"https://github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/oot\" class=\"post-link\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Oot</a>. If we are handing repo keys to autonomous models, version control needs to act as a court for code rather than a dumb text patcher.</p>\n\n        <p>Full case study: <a href=\"../projects/oot.html\" class=\"post-link\">Oot: a court for code</a></p>\n      "
  },
  "simplicity-is-the-new-sophistication": {
    "slug": "simplicity-is-the-new-sophistication",
    "title": "Simplicity is the new sophistication",
    "tag": "DEV · ARCHITECTURE",
    "date": "AUG 31, 2026",
    "desc": "How an 8GB downloads folder turned into a bloated 35k-line MCP server, the rate limit hell of early agent tools, and stripping it down for the stateless v5 era.",
    "withProgress": true,
    "withCopyMarkdown": true,
    "backHref": "/blog.html",
    "backLabel": "Back to blog",
    "body": "\n        <p>I started building <a href=\"https://github.com/kridaydave/File-Organizer-MCP\" class=\"post-link\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">File-Organizer-MCP</a> after watching a NetworkChuck video about Model Context Protocol and Docker's MCP registry. I looked over at my desktop and noticed my Downloads folder had accumulated 8 gigabytes of unorganized screenshots, random zip archives, physics PDFs, and installer binaries.</p>\n\n        <p>I wanted an agent to clean it up. But when I looked at the existing filesystem tools, they were built around dumb, low-level primitives: <code>read_file</code>, <code>move_file</code>, <code>list_directory</code>.</p>\n\n        <h2>The token trap of early MCP tools</h2>\n\n        <p>The early MCP era had a massive flaw: it was brutal for anyone on a free or limited API budget.</p>\n\n        <p>If you give an LLM an 8GB folder with 500 files and tell it to organize them using primitive tools, here is what happens:</p>\n\n        <ol>\n          <li>The model calls <code>list_directory</code> and dumps 500 filenames into the context window.</li>\n          <li>It calls <code>read_file</code> on file #1 (5,000 tokens).</li>\n          <li>It calls <code>create_directory</code> for category A.</li>\n          <li>It calls <code>move_file</code> to move file #1.</li>\n          <li>It repeats this loop 500 times.</li>\n        </ol>\n\n        <p>Every single tool result bloated the context by 5,000 to 10,000 tokens. Prompt caching was barely starting out. Within twenty minutes, you burned through 300,000 tokens and slammed directly into provider rate limits.</p>\n\n        <p>I hit this exact wall while recording promotional demo videos for the project. I was using Claude to showcase the tool live, and Anthropic hit me with rate limits in the middle of recording. I was so annoyed by the limits that I decided not to edit them out. I kept the rate limit error in the final video. It broke standard marketing playbooks, but people loved seeing the real struggle.</p>\n\n        <p>The conclusion was obvious: <strong>let local code handle everything the AI could do except decide the intent.</strong> The model should never manage individual file pointers in a loop. It should call one high-level, atomic tool, let local Node.js code batch-process 500 files in 15 milliseconds, and return a clean summary diff. One round trip. 400 tokens total.</p>\n\n        <h2>The 35,000-line monster</h2>\n\n        <p>File-Organizer-MCP was my first real open source project. Because I was learning Node.js <code>fs</code> and TypeScript on the fly, I fell into the classic first-project trap: every time I had an idea, I wrote a new service class for it.</p>\n\n        <p>Before long, the <code>main</code> branch ballooned into nearly 35,000 lines of code across 27 distinct services:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n          <li><code>image-metadata.service.ts</code>: 1,403 lines</li>\n          <li><code>content-analyzer.service.ts</code>: 1,304 lines</li>\n          <li><code>categorizer.service.ts</code>: 1,246 lines</li>\n          <li><code>file-signatures.ts</code>: 1,044 lines</li>\n          <li><code>secure-file-reader.ts</code>: 855 lines</li>\n          <li><code>content-screening.service.ts</code>: 788 lines</li>\n          <li>Heavy binary dependencies like <code>mammoth</code> (Word document parser) and <code>pdf-parse</code>.</li>\n          <li>A 2,000-line <code>readers/</code> abstraction layer with custom factories, rate limiters, and audit log queues.</li>\n          <li>A stateful file watcher living directly inside the stdio MCP connection.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <p>The project worked, and users found it valuable (42 stars on GitHub, including one user who asked for external drive mount support). But maintaining it felt like pushing a boulder uphill. Fixing a path validation edge case required navigating three factory classes. When JEE prep picked up, I slapped a maintenance banner on the README and froze feature development.</p>\n\n        <h2>The vision was right, the implementation was fat</h2>\n\n        <p>Bundling <code>mammoth</code> and <code>pdf-parse</code> in an MCP package was heavy overkill for local developer tooling. But the underlying idea was not wrong. People do want content-aware organization, they just do not want a 50MB dependency tree that crashes on malformed docx tables.</p>\n\n        <p>When I came back to the project to prepare the <code>simplify-v5</code> PR, I decided to rewrite the architecture from scratch.</p>\n\n        <h2>The simplify-v5 rethink &amp; stateless MCP 2.0</h2>\n\n        <p>The new MCP 2.0 specification introduced stateless servers. The old 2025 protocol forced stateful session handshakes, persistent <code>Mcp-Session-Id</code> headers, and in-memory singletons that leaked file descriptors. MCP 2.0 replaced all of that with <code>server/discover</code>, request metadata in <code>_meta</code>, and clean response caching via <code>cacheHints</code>.</p>\n\n        <p>I structured the v5 refactor around three hard rules:</p>\n\n        <ol>\n          <li><strong>The 300-line ceiling:</strong> No single file in the repository can cross 300 lines. If a module grows beyond that, it gets split or refactored.</li>\n          <li><strong>Zero heavy parsers:</strong> Dropped <code>mammoth</code>, <code>pdf-parse</code>, and the 2,000-line reader framework. Replaced them with a single hardened <code>core/io/readFile()</code> function with TOCTOU guards and sensitive path checks.</li>\n          <li><strong>Deterministic grouping over bloated NLP:</strong> Rebuilt project-based organization using token rarity and Inverse Document Frequency (IDF). It scans plain text markers and identifier tokens in milliseconds with zero external ML dependencies.</li>\n          <li><strong>Decoupled background watcher:</strong> Moved scheduled directory watching out of the MCP server into a standalone CLI binary (<code>bin/file-organizer-watch.mjs</code>). The core stdio server stays completely stateless.</li>\n        </ol>\n\n        <h2>The Linux migration plot twist</h2>\n\n        <p>While finishing v5, I migrated my main setup from Windows to Linux. In the process, I forgot to back up my npm recovery codes. I lost access to the original npm account where the package was published.</p>\n\n        <p>It was frustrating, but honestly fitting. v5 is a complete rebirth of the project anyway. I created a new publisher account and am publishing the clean v5 package there.</p>\n\n        <h2>42 stars and the moonshot</h2>\n\n        <p>File-Organizer-MCP is the first project I ever built. 42 people starred it, gave feedback, and used it daily. My goal for this project has not changed: I want to make it the most reliable, widely downloaded file organizer MCP server in the ecosystem. It is a moonshot, but aim for the stars and land on the moon.</p>\n\n        <p>The lesson from v1 through v5 was clear: it is not that the original features were bad, it is that the implementation was fat. Real engineering is not about how many abstraction layers you can add. It is about how many you can strip away while making the tool faster and more dependable.</p>\n\n        <h2>Appendix: The v5 changelog</h2>\n\n        <p>Here is the raw changelog excerpt from the <code>chore/simplify-v5</code> release:</p>\n\n        <pre><code>## [5.0.0] - 2026-08-22\n\n### ⚠️ Breaking Changes\n- Scheduler is now a standalone bin (bin/file-organizer-watch.mjs). Core stdio server serves 21 tools.\n- Content-based tools deleted (organize_smart, organize_by_content). Dropped dependencies: pdf-parse, mammoth.\n- Stateless protocol era: clients on 2025-era handshake negotiate via dual-era support.\n\n### 🚀 MCP 2026-07-28 Protocol Support\n- Switched to @modelcontextprotocol/server@2.0.0.\n- Implements stateless MCP spec: no session handshake, server/discover negotiation, response caching (cacheHints ttlMs: 3600000).\n\n### 🧹 Simplification (simplify-v5)\n- No file over 300 lines across the entire codebase.\n- 27 services collapsed into core/{path,io,scan,categorize,organize,hash}.\n- 2,000-line readers/ framework replaced by core/io/readFile() with TOCTOU guards.\n- Schemas reduced from 22 to 4 files. RateLimiter deleted.\n- History logger appends directly behind in-process write chain with cross-process lockfile.</code></pre>\n\n        <p>Full case study: <a href=\"../projects/file-organizer-mcp.html\" class=\"post-link\">File Organizer MCP: one call instead of twenty</a></p>\n      "
  },
  "t4-cuda-update-01": {
    "slug": "t4-cuda-update-01",
    "title": "The t4, the tea and a bumble-bee",
    "tag": "CUDA · RESEARCH",
    "date": "SEP 6, 2026",
    "desc": "Five weeks of CUDA kernel research on free Colab T4s: measured wins, a falsified hypothesis, and a 0.5B model that learned to say I don't know.",
    "withProgress": true,
    "withCopyMarkdown": true,
    "backHref": "/blog.html",
    "backLabel": "Back to blog",
    "body": "\n        <p>Modern inference engines have a type. vLLM, Marlin, FlashInfer, they all assume Ampere or newer. They hardcode hardware primitives that Turing never had, things like <code>cp.async</code> for async memory copies and matrix shapes built for third-generation <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"Specialized matrix-math units inside the SM. A T4 carries 320 of them, but only second-generation ones.\">tensor cores</span>. Run them on a Tesla T4 and you get a crash, or worse, a silent fallback to plain PyTorch.</p>\n\n        <p>Meanwhile, the T4 is everywhere. Google Colab hands you one for free. AWS g4dn, GCP, Azure all rent them by the hour. It's a 2018 card: 40 <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"The GPU's repeating compute units. A T4 has 40 of them; everything you run is spread across these.\">SMs</span>, 320 tensor cores, 16 GB of GDDR6.</p>\n\n        <p>So the question we gave ourselves at Epoch was blunt: is a free, old datacenter card, oblivious to what we'd do with it, enough for real kernel research and real training? Not toy benchmarks. Measured kernels, honest baselines, results you can replicate. Five weeks in, with a bunch of procrastination and coffee, we have an answer, and it's mostly yes with two interesting exceptions.</p>\n\n        <h2>House rules</h2>\n\n        <p>Before any numbers, the house rules. Every number we print gets a tag at birth: MEASURED means a CUDA event on real silicon, ESTIMATED means a model applied to measured inputs, ARITHMETIC means pure byte counting where no kernel exists yet. Benchmarks stay blocked until correctness tests pass, because a fast kernel that computes wrong answers is just wrong, quickly. And every comparison prints its clock state, so a speedup can't hide inside a fan curve.</p>\n\n        <p>Everything we ran falls under a numbered hypothesis. The full register, so you can hold us to it:</p>\n\n        <table>\n          <thead>\n            <tr><th>#</th><th>Hypothesis</th></tr>\n          </thead>\n          <tbody>\n            <tr><td>H1</td><td>128-bit vector loads and swizzle math</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H2</td><td>Register double-buffering software pipeline</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H3</td><td>Fused FlashAttention-2 sub-tile FP16 kernel</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H4</td><td>Signed INT4 two's-complement LOP3 unpacking</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H5</td><td>70W power-aware occupancy capping</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H6</td><td>Fused backward GEMM + inline AdamW</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H7</td><td>Signed INT3 LOP3 dequantization</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H8</td><td>Software warp specialization split-K</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H9</td><td>FP8 emulation on FP16 tensor cores</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H10&ndash;16</td><td>Extended microarchitecture frontiers (parked)</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H17</td><td>Fused INT3 dequant + GEMV mega-kernel</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H20</td><td>Speculative decoding with an INT3 0.5B draft</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H22</td><td>INT3/INT4 QAT pipeline for Turing</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H23</td><td>1.58-bit ternary bit-serial LOP3 accumulation</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H24</td><td>In-register persistent KV-cache stashing</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H25</td><td>Constant-bank scale streaming for sub-byte dequant</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H27</td><td>Rotation-fused MXFP4 weights / FP16 activations GEMV</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H28</td><td>Position-aware speculative tree shaping</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H29</td><td>Ternary weights &times; INT4 activations serving kernels</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>H30</td><td>Compiler-generated warp specialization for pre-async GPUs</td></tr>\n          </tbody>\n        </table>\n\n        <h2>What worked</h2>\n\n        <p>Two ideas carried the whole stack, and both survived contact with real hardware.</p>\n\n        <p>The first is how you turn a 4-bit weight into a number a GPU can multiply. <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"Recovering the original float values from quantized integers. Quantization packs weights into 4-bit integers to save memory; dequantization unpacks them at compute time.\">Dequantization</span> is normally dull: unpack the nibble, convert integer to float, multiply by a scale. That integer-to-float conversion costs about 20 <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"The GPU's actual assembly language: the machine instructions that physically execute on the card.\">SASS</span> instructions. H4, our signed INT4 path, uses a trick instead: take the packed nibble and OR it into the <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"The part of a floating-point number that stores the significant digits. Stuffing bits here makes them appear as a usable value.\">mantissa</span> of a half-float preloaded with a magic exponent, 0x6400. The nibble wakes up inside a ready-made float, 1024 plus q, no conversion pipe involved. Twenty instructions drop to eight. The signed case is prettier: one <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"A single CUDA instruction that computes any bitwise function of three inputs, chosen by a lookup table. One cycle.\">LOP3</span> with lookup table 0x6A flips the sign bit and inserts the exponent in the same instruction. Bit-exact against known-answer vectors, 23 of 23 tests passing, and the INT3 variant pushes ~332 GB/s against the card's 320 GB/s spec. That last number is the sound of a kernel limited by physics, not by us.</p>\n\n        <p>The second, H6, is a training kernel. A normal backward pass writes gradients to <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"The GPU's main memory, 16 GB of GDDR6 here. Big but slow next to on-chip storage.\">DRAM</span>, the optimizer reads them back, updates the weights, writes them again. Count the bytes and every parameter costs 28 bytes of memory traffic per step. H6 keeps the gradient accumulator in <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"Storage physically inside the SM. Orders of magnitude faster than main memory, and tiny.\">registers</span> while the <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"General matrix multiply. The operation neural networks are made of.\">GEMM</span>'s inner loop runs, then applies <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"The standard optimizer that updates network weights during training.\">AdamW</span> in-register before anything touches memory. 22 bytes per parameter, a 21.4% cut, measured at 1.94x against eager PyTorch on the first card, and a compiled baseline is a gate we still owe this kernel. Then, because one T4 proving something proves nothing, we re-ran the whole suite on a second physical card. 2.02x. Same result, different silicon.</p>\n\n        <h2>The bug the tests missed</h2>\n\n        <p>Then came the bug that taught us more than any speedup did.</p>\n\n        <p>We wired the INT4 kernel into real generation and the model produced garbage. Not slightly worse text. Garbage. Token agreement with the fp16 model was 12%, which for a language model is a coin flip with extra steps.</p>\n\n        <p>Here's the confusing part: the kernel passed every test. Bit-exact against known answers, error gates green. The tests compared the kernel against a Python reference implementation, and the Python reference did the same math the kernel did. In the <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"The final stage of a kernel, where results are finalized after the main compute loop.\">epilogue</span>, we had folded the (&minus;1024 &minus; zero-point) &times; scale correction into an <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"Fused multiply-add: one instruction computing a&times;b+c, with no rounding between the multiply and the add.\">fp16 FMA</span>. That means (1024 + q) &times; scale gets rounded to fp16 before the 1024 ever cancels out. At magnitude 1024, fp16's <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"Unit in the last place: the gap between two adjacent floating-point numbers at a given magnitude. At 1024, fp16's gap is exactly 1.0.\">ulp</span> is 1.0, so every weight carries about 1.0 of absolute noise no matter how small its scale is. Across an inner dimension of 896 with real activations, the noise is the signal. The tests never caught it because the reference made the same rounding mistake. Same blind spot on both sides of the comparison.</p>\n\n        <p>The fix takes two sentences to describe. First, keep the unpack exact: accumulate S1 = &Sigma;(1024+q)&middot;a and S2 = &Sigma;a in fp32, then reconstruct the correction in the epilogue instead of folding it in early. Second, do the multiply in fp32, not fp16, because products at magnitude 1024 round with ulp &asymp; 1.0 and that noise survives the cancellation. The first change alone still left 13% error. After the second, 0.04% relative error against the exact reference.</p>\n\n        <p>The lesson is one we keep re-learning: a test is only as good as the independence of its reference. Ours wasn't.</p>\n\n        <h2>The hypothesis we loved</h2>\n\n        <p>Every lab has the hypothesis it loves. H5 was ours.</p>\n\n        <p>The idea was clean. A T4 is passively cooled on a 70W budget, and if you launch kernels at 100% <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"How much of the SM's compute resources are actively in use. More is not always better.\">occupancy</span>, the card heats, clocks sag from 1590 MHz toward 950, and you lose throughput to the fan curve. So cap occupancy at 25% during heavy prefill, let the card breathe, and the <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"The GPU's maximum designed clock speed, reached when power and thermal headroom allow.\">boost clock</span> stays locked. In July this verified beautifully. A microbenchmark at full occupancy sagged to 1193 MHz; the capped version held 1590 and finished sooner. We were sure we'd found a free lunch specific to power-starved cards.</p>\n\n        <p>Five weeks later, under real <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"Group Relative Policy Optimization. A reinforcement learning method where the model generates several answers per question and learns from the spread of their rewards.\">GRPO</span> training load, it died. We locked the clock at 1590 with nvidia-smi, started training, and watched the card's own sensors report 1485 MHz at 66 of 70 watts, throttle reason SW_POWER_CAP. The driver accepted our lock and then couldn't honor it, because the card didn't have the watts. The moment we killed the training process, clocks snapped back to 1590 at 44W idle. The villain was never thermals. It's the power budget itself.</p>\n\n        <p>Did the capping at least help? Step time with the lock: 13.1 seconds. Without: 12.9 to 13.2. Statistically identical. For sustained training on Colab T4s, H5 is dead. It might still hold for bursty kernels, and it might hold on hosts with different power profiles, so the register entry says exactly that instead of a flat \"false\". But the version of H5 we loved, the one that made our microbenchmarks look brilliant, is gone.</p>\n\n        <p>This is the part of the method we'd defend even though it cost us our favorite idea. The register doesn't grade on effort. The hypothesis made a promise about real workloads, real workloads said no, and the entry now says falsified, with a scope note. Negative results are cheap to skip and expensive to fake.</p>\n\n        <h2>Where INT4 breaks</h2>\n\n        <p>The second exception starts with a boring profiling number. Generation is 85.4% of a GRPO training step. Quantize the <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"The generation phase of RL training, where the model produces answers that later get scored.\">rollouts</span> and training gets cheaper, obviously. The kernel was already verified, so we wired a scope that quantizes the weights when rollout starts and hands everything back to fp16 before the backward pass. Thirty steps on <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"Grade-school math word problems. The standard benchmark for RL with checkable answers.\">GSM8K</span> ran clean: zero out-of-memory errors, 13.36 GB peak. Mechanically, a triumph.</p>\n\n        <p>Then we looked at the <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"The score a generated answer earns. For math, usually just: was the final number right?\">rewards</span>. The fp16 baseline averaged 0.292 over 30 steps. The INT4 run averaged 0.025, roughly a twelfth of that. The model didn't crash and didn't ramble. It produced fluent answers to math problems and got them wrong at a rate that ended the experiment.</p>\n\n        <p>Here's what happened. In greedy decoding, the model picks the single most likely token, and if quantization noise is small, that pick usually stays the same. That's the regime our 1.20x speedup lived in. RL training is different: rollouts <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"Sampling from the model's probability distribution with added randomness, so it explores instead of always taking the top token.\">sample with temperature</span>, deliberately exploring the distribution instead of riding its peak. Quantization noise doesn't just nudge the peak, it reshapes the whole distribution, and the rollout explores the reshaped one. The reward function scores answers from a model that only exists during training. Naive <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"Each weight snaps to its nearest 4-bit level, with no error correction. The simplest quantization there is.\">round-to-nearest</span> quantization is fine for chat and fatal for chain-of-thought math.</p>\n\n        <p>A quieter finding hid in the same run. Single-sequence decode, batch size one, was 1.20x faster in INT4. Batched rollouts at 64 completions went the other way: 19.5 seconds per step versus 13.0 for fp16. At batch 64, <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"NVIDIA's hand-tuned matrix math library. It gets to use the tensor cores, which our custom GEMV path cannot.\">cuBLAS</span> gets the tensor cores, and our <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"Matrix-vector multiply: the batch-size-1 case of GEMM. Our kernel's home turf.\">GEMV</span> threadblocks, which parallelize along the batch, have no tensor cores and serialize across 40 SMs. Small batch: our kernel wins. Large batch: silicon wins. The regime split isn't a bug, it's the shape of the card.</p>\n\n        <p>This is the finding we'd put on the lab's door, if we had a door. Quantization papers evaluate with perplexity and greedy accuracy. Quantize a policy, and the metric that matters is reward under exploration, a metric that can collapse while every conventional one looks green. Nobody hits this failure on an H100, because nobody has to care about rollout cost there. The T4 forced the question, and the question had a real answer: quantized rollouts need calibration or a hybrid that keeps sensitive layers in fp16 before they go anywhere near RL.</p>\n\n        <h2>Speculative decoding, twice</h2>\n\n        <p><span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"A small model quickly guesses several tokens ahead and the big model checks all the guesses in one pass. Correct guesses are free speed.\">Speculative decoding</span> is the oldest idea in the stack, and it took us two builds to get right.</p>\n\n        <p>The first build was textbook. A 0.5B draft model in our INT4 format proposes up to K tokens, the 1.5B target verifies them in one forward pass, and both fit in the T4's 16 GB at the same time. The algorithm worked: at K=3, 64.2% of drafts were accepted, 2.93 tokens per target forward pass, with 100% output fidelity, the target model certifies every token so the answers can't drift. Wall-clock speedup: 0.97x. Slower than doing nothing.</p>\n\n        <p>The gap between 2.93 tokens per pass and 0.97x wall clock was glue. Each round made K+1 separate kernel calls from Python, and host-side launch overhead cost about 25 milliseconds per round, on a card where the actual math took less. The GPU was never the bottleneck. Our plumbing was.</p>\n\n        <p>So we rebuilt it as one engine. A preallocated static <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"The model's running memory of past tokens. Ours rolls back with a pointer instead of copying tensors.\">KV cache</span> that rolls back with an O(1) pointer move instead of tensor slicing, about 14,000x faster on that operation. A prompt-lookup drafter that reuses the user's own text to propose tokens, zero forward passes, 0.0062 milliseconds per proposal. A draft head that borrows the target model's own output layer, 98% fewer parameters to store. One loop, explicit positioning, no round trips.</p>\n\n        <p>Measured on the same card: 26.6 tok/s alone, 39.3 tok/s with the engine. A 1.48x wall-clock speedup, and per-prompt it ranged from 1.14x to 2.16x depending on how predictable the text was. The lesson generalizes further than this project: an algorithmic win is not a wall-clock win until the glue is gone, and on cheap hardware, glue is most of what you have.</p>\n\n        <h2>The demo</h2>\n\n        <p>All of that rides on one question: does the stack train anything real? For the demo we picked a behavior instead of a benchmark. We wanted the smallest model that knows when it doesn't know.</p>\n\n        <p>The recipe: <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\" data-term=\"A 0.5-billion-parameter model, small enough to run on a phone. Our test subject.\">Qwen2.5-0.5B</span>, 30 GRPO steps, and a balanced dataset: half answerable arithmetic and facts, half impossible-premise traps. Who was president of the Atlantic Ocean in 1850? What year did Atlantis fall on Venus? The base model hallucinated through 93.3% of the traps. It would invent an answer to anything, confidently.</p>\n\n        <p>Thirty steps later: abstention jumped from 6.7% to 53.3%, an 8x increase in saying \"I don't know\" to impossible questions. Hallucinations cut in half. Factual accuracy on the answerable questions held, 73.3% versus 76.7% before. And the whole run cost 103 seconds of wall clock and 8.47 GB of VRAM. Under 9 GB, on a free card, in under two minutes, a model learned a behavior people usually reserve for alignment budgets.</p>\n\n        <p>It's a small result and we're proud of it anyway. Not because 53.3% abstention is a record, but because every part of it ran on the kernels described above. The stack isn't decoration. It trained the thing.</p>\n\n        <aside class=\"post-note\">\n          <p>A note on the ones that didn't make it. Early on we planned three persona models: Albert at roughly 30B, Theo at 12B, and Eli at 4B. Only Eli was ever realistically in scope, and even that died fast, for two unglamorous reasons. The team is busy students and AI agents, and a taste judge needs a human with our taste, which the agents don't have and the students don't have time for. And we pivoted to kernel research instead, because we like math and the kernels were winning. Every week someone ships a new open model trained on hardware we will never touch, and it's hard not to wonder what we would have found in that track if we weren't compute and fund bound. We survive off of free tiers but strive ever the further to make a difference and prove a point.</p>\n        </aside>\n\n        <h2>So, do you need an H100?</h2>\n\n        <p>For some things, yes. The persona track died of compute, and no amount of cleverness fixes that. But for kernel research, the answer we found is no. The T4 can't hide anything. No headroom to waste, no thermal mass to absorb sloppy kernels, no budget to outsource your thinking to. Every inefficiency shows up on the meter, which makes it a strange and honest teacher. The big cards let you skip questions. This one makes you answer them.</p>\n\n        <p>Five weeks, two physical cards, one dead hypothesis, one dead model track, and a 0.5B model that learned to say \"I don't know.\" The register is public, the numbers are tagged, and the repo is linked below. Turns out the bumblebee flies fine.</p>\n\n        <p><a href=\"https://github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/t4-cuda\" class=\"post-link\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Epoch-AI-Lab/t4-cuda on GitHub</a></p>\n      "
  },
  "v5-0-0-is-live": {
    "slug": "v5-0-0-is-live",
    "title": "v5.0.0 is live",
    "tag": "DEV · RELEASE",
    "date": "SEP 1, 2026",
    "desc": "How an accidental flood of stealth Ox-Alpha tokens turned into a fat-to-fit rewrite of File-Organizer-MCP, deleting thousands of lines into v5.0.0.",
    "withProgress": true,
    "withCopyMarkdown": true,
    "backHref": "/blog.html",
    "backLabel": "Back to blog",
    "body": "\n        <p>v5.0.0 of <a href=\"https://github.com/kridaydave/File-Organizer-MCP\" class=\"post-link\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">File-Organizer-MCP</a> is officially live on npm. But the real reason this release exists is basically an accident of timing.</p>\n\n        <p>A few days ago, Z.ai quietly dropped their GLM-5.3-Flash model under stealth as Ox-Alpha. Along with it came a massive flood of free tokens. At the time, I was locked in on building <a href=\"https://github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/blueline\" class=\"post-link\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Blueline</a> in Rust, but when you are sitting on a mountain of free inference, it starts burning a hole in your pocket. I wanted an excuse to see what the model could actually do on messy, real-world code.</p>\n\n        <p>Around the same time, the whole timeline was losing its mind over Bun using Claude to rewrite 500,000 lines of Zig into Rust for $165,000. I looked at that and thought, why shouldn't I do a rewrite of my own? Except I did not need a $165,000 bill or a Rust ego trip nor did i have that. What I actually needed was to open up my first real open-source repo and aggressively gut the over-engineered TypeScript Frankenstein I built when I was first learning.</p>\n\n        <h2>Joining the cool kids club</h2>\n\n        <p>There is this running joke in tech that senior engineers measure their value by how much code they delete. You hear people talk about PRs with negative ten thousand lines like it is the official gateway into the cool kids club.</p>\n\n        <p>Except in my case, there was no 20-year staff engineer carefully pruning a monorepo. It was just a degenerate vibe-coder larping as a dev, feeding messy prompts into Ox-Alpha at 2 AM and watching thousands of lines of boilerplate get deleted in real time.</p>\n\n        <p>And honestly, it felt incredible. The original repo had ballooned into nearly 35,000 lines spread across 27 separate services. When you are learning Node and TypeScript on the fly, your immediate instinct is to create a brand new service class for every single shower thought. You wrap basic filesystem calls in three layers of architecture because you think that is what serious software looks like. Deleting whole directories of that fluff and watching the codebase shrink down to raw, focused tools was pure relief.</p>\n\n        <h2>Fuck around and find out, carefully</h2>\n\n        <p>I didn't have a bunch of users ready with machetes on my neck. It was just a fat-to-fit transformation for the codebase.</p>\n\n        <p>At the same time, this isn't a frontend web app where a bug just means a button renders funny. An MCP server has permission to move real files on your actual hard drive. It was mainly the fear of me being responsible for someone running a <code>rm -rf /*</code> on their machine, basically. If a filesystem tool glitches, it eats your documents or overwrites a project you spent three months building.</p>\n\n        <p>I was so paranoid about security and regressions that I had automated path traversal fuzzing and race-condition gates running on every change. I am one of the 7% gigachads who use Linux (arch btw) so people on Mac and Windows might not get the same native love, but that's what GitHub issues are for :/</p>\n\n        <h2>The anticlimactic npm saga</h2>\n\n        <p>Right before shipping, I thought I had hit a genuine wall. During my migration from Windows to Linux, I managed to lose access to my original npm account. I had already accepted my fate. I assumed the account was gone forever, that I would have to fork my own package, publish v5 under some garbage alternate name, and strand existing users on 3.4.2.</p>\n\n        <p>I had mentally prepared for a whole administrative headache. Then I hit \"forgot password\", checked my email, and got the account back in thirty seconds lol. No drama, no fork, same package name.</p>\n\n        <h2>Your code isn't as important as you think</h2>\n\n        <p>If there is one piece of advice I took away from this whole rewrite, it is that your code isn't as important as you think.</p>\n\n        <p>When you start out, you fall in love with your own complexity. You get emotionally attached to your 27 services, your clever helper classes, and your shiny abstractions. You convince yourself that writing more lines of code means you are doing real engineering. But nobody is framing your TypeScript files on their wall.</p>\n\n        <p>Your agents don't think that your 15 abstractions for a simple <code>read_file()</code> are cool, they'll shame you if they had access to social media. The question you should ask isn't \"Can I afford to make my code simpler\", the question is \"Can you afford not to?\"</p>\n\n        <p>v5.0.0 is live on npm. 22 tools, zero session baggage, and a codebase lean enough that I can actually read it without getting a headache.</p>\n\n        <div class=\"project-cmd\" data-copy=\"npm install -g file-organizer-mcp\" title=\"Click to copy\">\n          <code>npm install -g file-organizer-mcp</code>\n          <button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy install command\">\n            <svg class=\"icon-copy\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"13\" height=\"13\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect width=\"14\" height=\"14\" x=\"8\" y=\"8\" rx=\"2\" ry=\"2\"/><path d=\"M4 16c-1.1 0-2-.9-2-2V4c0-1.1.9-2 2-2h10c1.1 0 2 .9 2 2\"/></svg>\n            <svg class=\"icon-check\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"13\" height=\"13\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><polyline points=\"20 6 9 17 4 12\"/></svg>\n          </button>\n        </div>\n\n        <p>Point your client at it and go clean something up.</p>\n      "
  }
};

export const PROJECTS = {
  "blueline": {
    "slug": "blueline",
    "title": "Approve the change, not the download",
    "tag": "BLUELINE · CASE STUDY",
    "date": "RUST · CLI · 2026",
    "desc": "Blueline case study: sandboxed package extraction, delta reviews against verified baselines, and fail-closed supply chain security for coding agents.",
    "github": "https://github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/blueline",
    "withProgress": true,
    "withCopyMarkdown": false,
    "backHref": "/index.html#projects",
    "backLabel": "All projects",
    "body": "\n        <p>Blueline is a supply chain security gate for package installs, written in Rust. It exists because of one sentence: package managers treat installs as transport: fetch, unpack, run. Blueline adds the step they skip.</p>\n\n        <pre><code>npx blueline install express@4.21.2</code></pre>\n\n        <h2>The problem</h2>\n\n        <p>Your coding agent runs <code>npm install</code> dozens of times a day. Each one pulls tarballs off the registry and executes whatever install scripts came along for the ride. Nobody on earth diffs express 4.21.1 against 4.21.2 when an agent fires the command in a loop.</p>\n\n        <p>Attackers know this. The TanStack router worm shipped malicious npm packages carrying valid SLSA L3 provenance. In 2021, ua-parser-js was hijacked and three malicious versions published with a credential stealer in the preinstall script. The fix window was four hours. Agents compress that window to minutes.</p>\n\n        <h2>How it works</h2>\n\n        <p>Three steps, in order, before anything touches your machine:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n          <li><strong>Sandboxed extraction.</strong> The release tarball is unpacked in isolation, with guards against path traversal, symlinks, and decompression bombs.</li>\n          <li><strong>Delta calculation.</strong> The new release is diffed, at line level, against your last verified baseline (stored locally in SQLite). Approved versions become the baseline; the next release only has to explain its delta.</li>\n          <li><strong>Approval gate.</strong> A review card renders the change before it lands. High-risk releases block outright; the install halts until a human decides.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <pre><code>BLUELINE REVIEW CARD\n─────────────────────────────────────────\npackage:    express\nversion:    4.21.2\nprevious:   4.21.1 (last known-clean)\n\ndelta:      +3 files, -1 file\nsize:       +1.2 KB\nauthor:     expressjs (verified)\npermissions: no new binaries\ninstall script: none\n\nchanged:    lib/router/index.js (3 lines)\n            lib/router/layer.js (1 line, comment only)\n            package.json (version bump)\n\nverdict:    ▶ LOW RISK\n\n[a]pprove · [h]old · [d]iff</code></pre>\n\n        <p>Around that core loop:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n          <li><code>blueline mcp</code>: an MCP server, so agents call the review themselves instead of bypassing it</li>\n          <li><code>blueline ci</code>: a lockfile diff scanner for PRs (<code>package-lock.json</code>, <code>Cargo.lock</code>, <code>requirements.txt</code>), plus a GitHub Action</li>\n          <li>policy-as-code via <code>blueline.toml</code>: advisory thresholds, allowlists, blocklists</li>\n          <li>OSV and GitHub Advisory checks, SLSA/PEP 740 provenance surfacing</li>\n          <li>multi-registry: npm, PyPI (<code>--ecosystem pypi</code>), crates.io</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h2>Why you can trust the installer itself</h2>\n\n        <p>A supply chain tool delivered through the same supply chain it criticizes should hold itself to its own standard:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n          <li>Every binary publishes as an npm package with provenance attestation, and SHA256SUMS attach to each GitHub release.</li>\n          <li>A smoke gate runs before shims publish: the actual shipped artifact must review a real package end-to-end through the npm path. If the artifact can't do its one job, nothing ships.</li>\n          <li>Our own composite Action reviews our own <code>package-lock.json</code> on every pull request.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <p>The scanner also fails closed. Any doubt in parsing, extraction, or verification errors out rather than guessing. Mutation tests are pointed at those boundaries so a regression fails CI instead of failing you.</p>\n\n        <h2>Honest limits</h2>\n\n        <p>First installs have no baseline to diff against, so they lean harder on advisories, provenance, and static scanning. Heuristics catch common obfuscation routes but are not proof against everything. If you want lower friction while bootstrapping baselines, there's an <code>allow_unreviewed_baseline</code> rule in <code>blueline.toml</code>.</p>\n\n        <h2>Try it</h2>\n\n        <pre><code>npm i -g blueline\nblueline review lodash@4.17.21</code></pre>\n\n        <p>Prebuilt binaries for Linux (x64 glibc and musl, arm64), macOS (x64, arm64) and Windows (x64, arm64). No Rust toolchain required.</p>\n\n        <p>Repo and docs: <a href=\"https://github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/blueline\" class=\"post-link\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/blueline</a></p>\n\n        <p>Related essay: <a href=\"../posts/blueline-v0-2-0.html\" class=\"post-link\">Your agent installs code nobody reads</a></p>\n      "
  },
  "file-organizer-mcp": {
    "slug": "file-organizer-mcp",
    "title": "One call instead of twenty",
    "tag": "FILE ORGANIZER MCP · CASE STUDY",
    "date": "TYPESCRIPT · MCP · V5.0.0",
    "desc": "File Organizer MCP case study: one atomic organize call with dry-run safety gates, rollback, and 8-layer path validation for LLM agents.",
    "github": "https://github.com/kridaydave/File-Organizer-MCP",
    "withProgress": true,
    "withCopyMarkdown": false,
    "backHref": "/index.html#projects",
    "backLabel": "All projects",
    "body": "\n        <p>File Organizer MCP is a local-first filesystem server for LLM agents, and my most-used open source project with 43 stars, six forks, and enough real usage to force four major versions. Its core bet: generic filesystem MCP servers make assistants chain twenty primitive calls (read, write, rename, mkdir…), and every extra call costs tokens and invites a path error. This server replaces the chain with one atomic operation.</p>\n\n        <pre><code>npx file-organizer-mcp --setup</code></pre>\n\n        <h2>One atomic call</h2>\n\n        <p>The centerpiece is <code>organize_files()</code>: a single call that runs the whole scan → categorize → plan → move pipeline atomically. If something fails partway, the operation rolls back instead of leaving your Downloads folder half-shuffled. Conflict strategies are explicit: <code>rename</code>, <code>skip</code>, or <code>overwrite</code> with backup, and dry-run previews show the plan before anything moves.</p>\n\n        <p>The organization itself is smarter than folder shuffling:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n          <li>12+ file type categories, with per-type strategies</li>\n          <li>Photos organized by EXIF date into <code>YYYY/MM/DD</code> folders; music by ID3 tags into <code>Artist/Album/Title</code></li>\n          <li>Duplicate detection via SHA-256 content hashing, scored by location, name quality, and age before you choose what to delete</li>\n          <li>A security-screening workflow that flags sensitive metadata, like GPS coordinates in photos, for redaction</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h2>The safety model</h2>\n\n        <p>Handing an agent filesystem access is the whole point and the whole risk, so the server is paranoid by construction:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n          <li>Whitelist-based directory access; external drives require an explicit <code>allowExternalVolumes</code> opt-in</li>\n          <li>System paths always blocked, with 8-layer path validation, symlink attack protection, and TOCTOU mitigation</li>\n          <li>Resource limits so a runaway agent can't DoS the machine</li>\n          <li>Undo and history tools, so mistakes are reversible</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h2>What v5 taught me</h2>\n\n        <p>v5.0.0 was a ground-up rewrite: stateless MCP protocol, 22 tools, a registry where adding a tool means one file and one line. The interesting lessons weren't about files. They were about shipping: a case-collision bug that ate files, a dead error branch, and required checks pointing at a deleted workflow, all caught in merge week. I wrote the full shipping log in the essays below.</p>\n\n        <p>There's also a companion <code>file-organizer-watch</code> daemon for cron-based scheduled organizing, keeping the stdio server itself stateless.</p>\n\n        <h2>Try it</h2>\n\n        <pre><code>npm i -g file-organizer-mcp\nnpx file-organizer-mcp --setup</code></pre>\n\n        <p>The setup wizard auto-detects Claude Desktop, Cursor, Windsurf, and Cline. Then just ask your assistant to \"organize my Downloads folder\". Requires Node.js 18+. Windows, macOS, and Linux.</p>\n\n        <p>Repo and docs: <a href=\"https://github.com/kridaydave/File-Organizer-MCP\" class=\"post-link\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/kridaydave/File-Organizer-MCP</a></p>\n\n        <p>Related essays: <a href=\"../posts/simplicity-is-the-new-sophistication.html\" class=\"post-link\">Simplicity is the new sophistication</a> · <a href=\"../posts/v5-0-0-is-live.html\" class=\"post-link\">v5.0.0 is live</a></p>\n      "
  },
  "oot": {
    "slug": "oot",
    "title": "A court for code",
    "tag": "OOT · CASE STUDY",
    "date": "RUST · VCS · 2026",
    "desc": "Oot case study: a code governance engine resolving intent disputes, visibility policies, and semantic merge conflicts for multi-agent workflows.",
    "github": "https://github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/oot",
    "withProgress": true,
    "withCopyMarkdown": false,
    "backHref": "/index.html#projects",
    "backLabel": "All projects",
    "body": "\n        <p>Oot is a governance engine for repos where multiple agents write code. The one-line pitch: <strong>git and Jujutsu track lines. Oot holds history so it can govern it.</strong></p>\n\n        <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/oot</code></pre>\n\n        <h2>The problem</h2>\n\n        <p>Agents now write many of the merges. Research Oot's README cites claims code from semantic conflicts is 26x more likely to be buggy. Meanwhile git's permissions are repo-wide (file-level privacy is awkward), branches and PRs are heavyweight for machine-speed work, and text-based merges can't catch conflicts about meaning rather than lines. Two agents can edit adjacent lines without a textual conflict and still produce a program that does neither thing either of them claimed.</p>\n\n        <h2>Changes, not commits</h2>\n\n        <p>The core abstraction is the <strong>change</strong>: a delta between two snapshots, judged without a checkout or working tree. History lives natively in <code>.oot/</code>, a bare git object database plus a change-id DAG, with parents referenced by change IDs, not commit SHAs.</p>\n\n        <p>On top of that sit the governance primitives:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n          <li><strong>Visibility policy.</strong> Path- or branch-level rules like <code>private-to</code>, <code>embargo-until</code>, <code>public</code>. Privacy is treated as policy, not crypto.</li>\n          <li><strong>Intent.</strong> What a change claims to mean, checked against its actual effects.</li>\n          <li><strong>Dispute.</strong> Either visibility (a private path was touched) or meaning (two changes clash on intent).</li>\n          <li><strong>Docket.</strong> The record of the dispute, verdict, visibility, and embargo.</li>\n          <li><strong>Verdicts.</strong> <code>adjudicated</code>, <code>blocked</code>, <code>embargoed</code>, or <code>cloaked</code>.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h2>How adjudication works</h2>\n\n        <p>Meaning disputes come from a structural engine built on tree-sitter parse trees across Rust, Go, JavaScript, TypeScript, TSX, and Python. Because it needs no working tree, the engine can run inside an agent's memory isolate.</p>\n\n        <p>Git stays the interchange format. A change arrives from a human on git, an agent on Jujutsu, or a model in memory; Oot checks it and prints a docket. Export is byte-identical on round-trip, and GitHub is reached through that export, not a fork of git. Embargoed security fixes follow the pattern git and GitHub already use manually, per OSSF guidance.</p>\n\n        <pre><code>oot adjudicate --base-ref main --head-ref feature/auth --repo .\noot adjudicate --source jj --base-ref main --head-ref '@-'\noot update --dry-run\noot docket</code></pre>\n\n        <p>Exit code 0 means adjudicated; anything else is nonzero, so CI can gate on it the same way it gates on tests.</p>\n\n        <h2>Status</h2>\n\n        <p>Oot is a working seed that dogfoods itself: the repo's own changes are governed by Oot. Already shipped: git and Jujutsu ingestion, visibility policy with embargoes, meaning disputes, dockets, the native store, and import / record / log / status / update / gc / export with byte-identical round-trip and visibility filtering.</p>\n\n        <p>On the roadmap, deliberately held back until there are real users: hosted intent scoring (a model comparing claimed intent against the actual diff effect), quiet delivery of embargo notices to maintainers, and signature reuse on rewritten commits.</p>\n\n        <p>Repo: <a href=\"https://github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/oot\" class=\"post-link\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/Epoch-AI-Lab/oot</a></p>\n\n        <p>Related essay: <a href=\"../posts/multi-agent-git-chaos.html\" class=\"post-link\">Multi-agent coding loops will wreck your git history</a></p>\n      "
  }
};
